    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d32">

                <pb facs="0118" n="38" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg38"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/628b: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chairman of the American
                        Delegation</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-01-20T14:00:00-05:00">January 20, 1936—2
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>23. In a conversation on the naval situation, the President expressed the opinion
                    that as far as any new agreement which may be negotiated <pb facs="0119" n="39"
                        xml:id="pg39"/> at the Conference as now constituted is concerned, we should
                    still seek an agreement in the first instance within the tonnage limits in the
                    existing treaties, with an escape clause in the event Japan exceeds her existing
                    treaty limitations. You should, therefore, endeavor to make that your first
                    principle as the conversations proceed. The question of cruiser tonnage
                    increase, if insisted on, remains as previously outlined. In other words, you
                    should seek compensating decreases in other categories.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
